tesla,2026-02,324000,1279000,1240630,1470850,275400,405000,1,1,1,1,1,1,2026-10-15,"Tesla's own Cumulative Paid Robotaxi Miles chart: monthly values vector-extracted from the Q4-2025 and Q1-2026 update-deck PDFs (overlapping months agree within ~3k mi, the Q2-2026 deck's within ~8k; 658k end-2025, 1.717M end-Mar-2026). Texas driverless service only — the Q3-2025 call tracks Bay Area as a separate supervised series (1M+ mi by Oct 2025) and the chart's August 2025, about 11k mi, is too small to contain the Bay fleet. Read as fleet service miles incl. deadhead, matching the incident scope (None + In-Vehicle + Remote Commercial/Test); if 'paid' is literally passenger-on-board miles the true total is higher, so the cumulative band's top carries ~15% upside and its floor ~3% read noise; a month's band is 0.85x-1.25x its miles, within the difference of its cumulative band and the month before's. These months' miles may include trips with a safety monitor in the driver's seat (@robotaxi on X, Sep 4, 2025: 'Safety monitors are only in the driver's seat for trips that involve highway driving'; David Moss on X, Jan 28, 2026: 'This was also my 5th ride in a row with the supervisor in the drivers [seat]'), and a crash on such a trip, if it ran as FSD (Supervised), is reportable only under NHTSA's Level 2 rules (Third Amended SGO, Apr 24, 2025, Request No. 1.D: a fatality, a hospital transport, a struck vulnerable road user, an airbag deployment on any vehicle in the crash, or any vehicle's tow-away 'if the crash involved a subject vehicle equipped with an ADS') and goes to its Level 2 file, whose Tesla reports this page does not count: a possible pro-Tesla bias of unknown size that the no-monitor line avoids ([data/README.md, Tesla's no-monitor line](data/#teslas-no-monitor-line))."
