tesla,2026-04,374000,2091000,2028270,2404650,317900,467500,1,1,1,1,1,1,2026-10-15,"Tesla's own Cumulative Paid Robotaxi Miles chart: monthly values vector-extracted from the Q2-2026 update-deck PDF (13 points through Jun-2026, 2.440M end-Q2; overlapping months re-confirm the earlier decks' series within ~8k mi). Austin + Dallas/Houston (unsupervised Dallas/Houston launch Apr 18, 2026); Bay Area remains a separate supervised series. Read as fleet service miles incl. deadhead, matching the incident scope (None + In-Vehicle + Remote Commercial/Test); if 'paid' is literally passenger-on-board miles the true total is higher, so the cumulative band's top carries ~15% upside and its floor ~3% read noise. (Pre-pin activity-ratio estimates ran ~1.4-2.9x high for Q2's months, +28% on the end-Q2 cumulative — a utilization-led slowdown the fleet counts missed.) These months' miles may include trips with a safety monitor in the driver's seat (@robotaxi on X, Sep 4, 2025: 'Safety monitors are only in the driver's seat for trips that involve highway driving'; David Moss on X, Jan 28, 2026: 'This was also my 5th ride in a row with the supervisor in the drivers [seat]'), and a crash on such a trip, if it ran as FSD (Supervised), is reportable only under NHTSA's Level 2 rules (Third Amended SGO, Apr 24, 2025, Request No. 1.D: a fatality, a hospital transport, a struck vulnerable road user, an airbag deployment on any vehicle in the crash, or any vehicle's tow-away 'if the crash involved a subject vehicle equipped with an ADS') and goes to its Level 2 file, whose Tesla reports this page does not count: a possible pro-Tesla bias of unknown size that the no-monitor line avoids ([data/README.md, Tesla's no-monitor line](data/#teslas-no-monitor-line))."
