waymo,2026-06,18827345,277774761,275069379,281379379,17091585,21013105,1,1,1,1,1,1,2026-10-15,"US est., bottom-up on the Waymo hub's MEASURED Q2 (Sep 24, 2026 update: 271.329M rider-only miles thru Jun-2026; re-chained 2026-09-25): CA = CPUC Q2-2026 driverless actual 9,023,791 deployment + 660,877 pilot (CPUC's total driverless VMT incl. deadhead) x 1.0576, the hub-vs-CPUC ratio for the hub's CA counties in Q2 (Q1: 1.040) = 10.24M (±3%); Maricopa 3.78M and Travis 1.84M = the hub's measured Q2 county increments (11.57M / 5.28M) split by the prior monthly shape (±10%); Atlanta 1.17M = the hub's measured Q2 Fulton+DeKalb increment (3.25M) split by the prior monthly shape (±10%); + rider-only metros the hub does not count (Miami, Dallas, Houston, Orlando, Nashville; San Antonio passenger service halted Apr 20 to Sep 17 after flood strandings Apr 4 and Apr 20) 1.80M [1.05M, 3.00M]. Public freeway rides suspended all month (Waymo's recall filing 26E035000: 'Waymo resumed vehicle operations on freeway June 25, 2026'); Nashville open to all Jun 25; the CPUC pilot file carries the Ojai (Zeekr) trusted-tester rides. The Jun-2026 cumulative lands on the anchor: the hub's 271.329M plus 6.65M estimated in metros the hub does not count, less the 0.205M pre-series miles; its cumulative band is the hub total plus 4.0M to 10.2M for those metros, less the pre-series miles' range, 0.15M-0.26M ([data/README.md, Waymo anchors](data/#waymo-anchors)). An Atlanta double-count correction applied 2026-09-25 was withdrawn 2026-09-29: Waymo's corrected per-cell file (CSV4 v2, posted Sep 28, linked from waymo.com/safety/impact since Oct 4-6 (UTC) with release notes dated Oct 2 and uploaded Oct 5, which say the corrected CSV3 and CSV4 'should replace the files released on September 24'; the hub's CSV3 link still serves the Sep 24 file) lists each cell once and sums to the published county totals."
zoox,2026-06,280000,2719920,2511077,3163912,220000,356000,1,1,1,1,1,1,2026-10-15,"US est. (rebuilt 2026-10-27, one path 2026-11-03): California is CPUC's driverless miles x 1.19 (filed through September; July and August in the [CPUC Q3 driverless pilot report (ZIP)](https://www.cpuc.ca.gov/-/media/cpuc-website/divisions/consumer-protection-and-enforcement-division/documents/tlab/av-programs/av-pilot-2026q3.zip)); the rest, mostly Las Vegas, follows the smoothest path through Zoox's four statements, flat at 6,500.65 a day from December 2025 (no fleet count since ~65 Las Vegas vehicles in service on Sep 2; no pause, recall or halt found). It puts 3,083,678 lifetime miles at Aug 5, inside 'more than 3 million miles on public roads' (Aug 5; Bloomberg, Las Vegas Sun) read as 3.00M-3.20M. End-July cumulative floor 2,880,077: 'more than 3 million' as of Jul 29-Aug 5, less the pre-series miles' high edge; 'more than' sets no ceiling. Bands: statement rounding, drift from the midpoint of Mar 25-Aug 5 and month noise ([data/README.md, Ghost months](data/#ghost-months); [data/README.md, Zoox anchors](data/#zoox-anchors))."
tesla,2026-07,279000,2719000,2612800,3126000,246000,320000,1,1,1,1,1,1,2026-10-15,"Tesla stopped charting miles from every mode after Q2: its Q3-2026 update deck (p8, 'Cumulative Unsupervised Robotaxi Miles') and its Robotaxi Safety Report page (tesla.com/robotaxi/safety, live Oct 21, 2026, which embeds weekly cumulative miles per city) count only driving with no safety monitor in the vehicle, including remote-operator assistance or takeover; Tesla does not say whether repositioning or employee rides are in them. That is a subset of the scope here (every Tesla operator mode, monitor aboard or not), so it sets the floor: July's no-monitor miles are 246,410 on the page (city sums interpolated to Jul 31 within its week) and 251,469 in the deck; floor 246,000. The central adds 33,000 monitor-aboard miles, June's measured remainder (the Q2 deck's 157,000 paid miles less the page's 123,823 no-monitor miles), held flat; the top, 320,000, assumes the monitor-aboard share stayed at or below June's 21% (the higher reading x 1.27). The page's series reproduces Tesla's 'more than 380,000' unsupervised miles of Jul 22 (388,672 by that date). July launches: Miami driverless Jul 3, Tampa and Orlando Jul 21."
waymo,2026-07,19669546,297444307,291441059,304432211,16371680,23052832,1,1,1,1,1,1,2026-10-15,"US est., bottom-up extension on the hub basis (re-chained 2026-11-19): CA = CPUC Q3-2026 driverless actual 9,479,565 deployment + 552,166 pilot (CPUC's total driverless VMT incl. deadhead) x 1.0576, the hub-vs-CPUC ratio for the hub's CA counties in Q2 = 10.61M (±3%); Maricopa 3.95M (hub Q2 run-rate 3.86M/mo; public freeway rides back Jul 29 from Phoenix; Waymo's recall filing 26E035000 says freeway vehicle operations resumed Jun 25), Travis 1.85M, Atlanta 1.26M (hub Q2 run-rate 1.08M/mo) (±25%); + uncounted metros 2.0M [0.785M, 3.3M] (12 JUL SGO crashes in them ≈ 1.8M at the 6.6/M young-market crash rate; Denver/Las Vegas/San Diego/Tampa employee rider-only from ~Jul 8). San Diego had 0 of the 12 newer-metro reports. The low edge allows for early San Diego miles in the statewide filing ([data/README.md, Waymo anchors](data/#waymo-anchors)). SF ~1-hour pause Jul 18 (power outage); LA/SF Bay freeways back ~first week of Aug. Every Waymo total-distance figure for a date after Jun 2026 is a floor the series clears, the highest being 'more than 300 million fully autonomous miles' (Alphabet's Q3 remarks, Oct 28, 2026); the other later figures are floors too: 'more than 4,000' vehicles (TechCrunch, Sep 1; it already said 'around 4,000' on Jun 29; the Jun 13 recall covered 3,871 5th-gen vehicles) and 'more than half a million paid trips weekly' (Waymo, Sep 15, 2026; the same floor Waymo has stated since late March), while '4M+ mi/wk' was last stated Jun 24. Next anchor: the hub update with data thru Sep (~mid/late Dec)."
